Solid-state fermentation can also be used to fabricate 3D foam materials from mycelium. The process induces the aerial growth of hyphae on a lignocellulosic particulate substrate unlike a gel-based media used for fabricating 2D membranes. The agri-waste particles are inoculated with living mycelial tissue (pre-grown on agar plates), and the inoculated substrate is grown in rectangular molds (Fig.  2 a). The substrate is nutritionally supplemented with carbohydrate, calcium, and water to support mycelial growth [ 6 ]. The necessary conditions for aerial growth of hyphae include ideal temperature (~ 30 ºC), CO 2  content (50–70 k ppm), and humidity (40–99%) of the growth environment [ 62 ,  63 ]. The living mycelium first colonizes the substrate particles and subsequently undergoes aerial growth away from the substrate. The accumulation of CO 2  inside the substrate due to cellular respiration drives the hyphae to grow upward in search of oxygen-rich air. A constant air flow is provided to stimulate the aerial growth further, which directly impacts the bulk density of the foam [ 64 ]. Hyphal growth is deactivated by convective heat treatment in the oven. Mycelium-based foams exhibit network-like microstructures with randomly distributed hyphae (Fig.  2 b), like mycelium membranes. The hyphal diameter varies in the range from 1 to 3  μ m \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
