The overall mechanical behavior of the mycelium-based foams is non-linear both in tension and compression. Figure  6 a shows their stress–strain responses under uniaxial tension and compression, respectively. Mycelium foams exhibit a linear elastic response at small strains with nearly identical elastic modulus in tension and compression [ 6 ]. The elastic symmetry is characteristic of random fiber network materials, for which the elastic regime is dominated by the bending deformation of the fibers (i.e., hyphae) [ 83 ]. The elastic modulus of mycelium foam varies with the square of mycelium density, which further implies that mycelium foams behave like a bending-dominated cellular solid [ 6 ]. Beyond the elastic limit, the material yields and softens in both cases, with more pronounced softening in compression. The softening in tension involves linear strain hardening of the material due to plastic deformation of individual hyphae. Mycelium foams exhibit moderate ductility under tension before rupture. Failure does not always occur at the mid-surface of the specimen (Fig.  6 b, left), but rather occurs at the weakest zone due to microstructural heterogeneity and spatial fluctuations of hyphal network density. In contrast to tensile response, the compression softening is associated with the strain localization and the initiation of collapse band formation in the specimen (Fig.  6 b, right) due to buckling deformation of hyphae under compression [ 6 ]. Figure 6 a  Experimental stress–strain curves of a mycelium foam under tension and compression.  b  Ruptured dog-bone specimen after tension testing (left) and deformed configuration of a cuboid specimen under compression, exhibiting the formation of collapse band (right).  c  Stress–strain response of mycelium foam under cycling compression (c) single cycle (two different relative densities,  ρ s \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
